1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,335
Computational Skepticism, Exam Prep.

2
00:00:01,385 --> 00:00:03,413
Key Terms: The Limits of AI.

3
00:00:03,463 --> 00:00:08,607
Thirty-four terms for what AI tools cannot
do, however large they get, and what the

4
00:00:08,657 --> 00:00:10,338
human supervisor does about it.

5
00:00:10,388 --> 00:00:14,840
Knowing them lets you say exactly where a
validated system can still fail.

6
00:00:15,990 --> 00:00:16,191
Hallo.

7
00:00:16,241 --> 00:00:17,695
This is Liam, in for Bear.

8
00:00:17,745 --> 00:00:21,454
A newcomer believes that more data will
remove the gap between what an AI system

9
00:00:21,504 --> 00:00:22,958
learned and the world it meets.

10
00:00:23,008 --> 00:00:23,710
It will not.

11
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:27,720
More data moves the edge of the sample
outward, and the gap sits at that edge.

12
00:00:27,770 --> 00:00:31,229
This film goes through thirty-four terms
for those limits, each one shown in a

13
00:00:31,279 --> 00:00:31,480
picture.

14
00:00:31,830 --> 00:00:34,802
Six labels sit in the corners of the
pictures that follow.

15
00:00:34,852 --> 00:00:38,099
In the book text: the course text uses and
explains the term.

16
00:00:38,149 --> 00:00:42,769
In the glossary: the term is on the course
term list, but the text does not explain

17
00:00:42,819 --> 00:00:43,043
it.

18
00:00:43,093 --> 00:00:46,065
Computed: the numbers are worked out on
screen from stated inputs.

19
00:00:46,115 --> 00:00:50,461
Simulation: a made-up run with a fixed
seed, so it comes out the same every time.

20
00:00:50,511 --> 00:00:53,208
Illustrative data: numbers chosen to show
the idea, not measured.

21
00:00:53,258 --> 00:00:56,230
Conceptual: a drawing of the idea, with no
data behind it.

22
00:00:56,580 --> 00:01:00,864
This is a map, a table of thirty-four
terms in four groups.

23
00:01:00,914 --> 00:01:03,392
The first group is the three limits.

24
00:01:03,442 --> 00:01:06,642
Its terms are capability scaling,
functionalist objection, data-world gap,

25
00:01:06,692 --> 00:01:09,531
categorical versus frequent, meaning,
semantic work and intentionality.

26
00:01:09,581 --> 00:01:12,420
It covers what no amount of scaling
removes.

27
00:01:12,770 --> 00:01:16,023
The second group is why the gap is real.

28
00:01:16,073 --> 00:01:19,327
Its terms are Hume's induction problem,
complementarity, categorical boundary,

29
00:01:19,377 --> 00:01:22,263
structural limit, Mediocristan,
Extremistan, turkey problem and black

30
00:01:22,313 --> 00:01:22,630
swan.

31
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:28,503
Together they explain why more data cannot
reach the part of the world the data left

32
00:01:28,553 --> 00:01:28,870
out.

33
00:01:29,220 --> 00:01:31,878
The third group is tests and arguments.

34
00:01:31,928 --> 00:01:36,133
Its terms are validation set, distribution
shift, wrong tests, Turing's argument,

35
00:01:36,183 --> 00:01:39,614
Chinese Room, overclaiming, operational
stance, skepticism as method and

36
00:01:39,664 --> 00:01:40,775
skepticism as safety.

37
00:01:40,825 --> 00:01:45,803
It covers what tests and two famous
arguments settle, and what they leave

38
00:01:45,853 --> 00:01:46,190
open.

39
00:01:46,540 --> 00:01:49,141
The fourth group is practice and
delegation.

40
00:01:49,191 --> 00:01:52,550
It holds Epic Sepsis Model, circular
signal, override infrastructure,

41
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:55,959
fictitious override, stop condition,
authority to refuse, delegate freely,

42
00:01:56,009 --> 00:01:58,990
delegate and verify, do not delegate, and
accountability.

43
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:04,671
This is what the supervisor, the human in
charge of the system, does about the

44
00:02:04,721 --> 00:02:05,050
limits.

45
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:07,776
A model's error as its training points
grow.

46
00:02:07,826 --> 00:02:11,415
Capability scaling means making a model
bigger or giving it more data.

47
00:02:11,465 --> 00:02:15,963
In this simulated run the dark line, error
inside the data's range, falls to zero

48
00:02:16,013 --> 00:02:17,176
point zero zero two.

49
00:02:17,226 --> 00:02:21,724
For example, the makers of a résumé
screener feed it far more past résumés, or

50
00:02:21,774 --> 00:02:25,060
swap in a larger model, and expect its
mistakes to fall.

51
00:02:25,410 --> 00:02:28,360
The grey dashed line is a prediction, not
a measurement.

52
00:02:28,410 --> 00:02:32,860
The functionalist objection is the reply
that a limit is a high error rate, so

53
00:02:32,910 --> 00:02:34,360
scaling shrinks it to nothing.

54
00:02:34,410 --> 00:02:37,360
It predicts error outside the data falls
the same way.

55
00:02:37,410 --> 00:02:40,960
For example, told that its job-matching
agent misjudges unusual career paths, a

56
00:02:41,010 --> 00:02:44,860
vendor replies that a larger version will
see enough of them to stop.

57
00:02:45,210 --> 00:02:49,479
The red line is the simulated error
outside the data, where the model had no

58
00:02:49,529 --> 00:02:50,055
training points.

59
00:02:50,105 --> 00:02:52,071
It stays near zero point eight six.

60
00:02:52,121 --> 00:02:56,390
That is the data-world gap, a model is
competent over its data, not over the

61
00:02:56,440 --> 00:02:56,678
world.

62
00:02:56,728 --> 00:02:59,845
For example, a salary estimator learned
only from software job postings.

63
00:02:59,895 --> 00:03:04,452
Asked about a nursing job, it still gives
a figure, with nothing in its data behind

64
00:03:04,502 --> 00:03:04,740
it.

65
00:03:05,090 --> 00:03:08,060
The term categorical versus frequent tells
the two lines apart.

66
00:03:08,110 --> 00:03:11,081
A frequent error shrinks with scale, like
the dark line.

67
00:03:11,131 --> 00:03:15,309
A categorical limit stays at the sample's
edge however much the model scales, like

68
00:03:15,359 --> 00:03:16,215
the red line.

69
00:03:16,265 --> 00:03:19,839
For example, a résumé parser that
sometimes misreads dates makes a frequent

70
00:03:19,889 --> 00:03:21,651
error, and more training fixes it.

71
00:03:21,701 --> 00:03:24,974
A job title invented after its data was
gathered is categorical.

72
00:03:25,024 --> 00:03:27,390
More of the old data never teaches it.

73
00:03:27,740 --> 00:03:29,519
Three boxes, system, world and supervisor.

74
00:03:29,569 --> 00:03:32,568
Meaning is what a symbol refers to in the
world.

75
00:03:32,618 --> 00:03:37,140
The system outputs the word stable, with
no link inside it to the patient's real

76
00:03:37,190 --> 00:03:37,445
state.

77
00:03:37,495 --> 00:03:38,969
The supervisor supplies the link.

78
00:03:39,019 --> 00:03:41,713
For example, an applicant-tracking system
marks a résumé qualified.

79
00:03:41,763 --> 00:03:46,286
Nothing inside it ties that word to a real
person's skills or a real job.

80
00:03:46,336 --> 00:03:47,810
The recruiter makes that link.

81
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:50,164
Three questions, each with a system's
answer.

82
00:03:50,214 --> 00:03:54,271
Semantic work is the supervisor mapping an
output's symbols to what they refer to

83
00:03:54,321 --> 00:03:56,911
here, and checking the answer fits the
question asked.

84
00:03:56,961 --> 00:03:59,258
In these illustrative rows, one of three
fits.

85
00:03:59,308 --> 00:04:03,952
For example, a job seeker asks an agent
whether she should apply for a role, and

86
00:04:04,002 --> 00:04:05,419
it lists the role's requirements.

87
00:04:05,469 --> 00:04:09,233
Her semantic work is noticing that answers
a different question, then matching each

88
00:04:09,283 --> 00:04:10,700
requirement to her own experience.

89
00:04:11,050 --> 00:04:13,003
One output, high risk, in two deployments.

90
00:04:13,053 --> 00:04:16,723
Intensive care takes it as risk of death,
billing as risk of non-payment.

91
00:04:16,773 --> 00:04:19,298
Intentionality is aboutness, what an
output is directed at.

92
00:04:19,348 --> 00:04:22,445
It follows the user, not a stable aim in
the system.

93
00:04:22,495 --> 00:04:25,307
For example, a tool labels an applicant a
strong match.

94
00:04:25,357 --> 00:04:28,168
To a hiring manager that is about whom to
interview.

95
00:04:28,218 --> 00:04:32,460
To the applicant using the same tool, it
is about whether to apply at all.

96
00:04:32,810 --> 00:04:36,528
Fourteen ticked days that resembled the
past, then a question mark for tomorrow.

97
00:04:36,578 --> 00:04:40,007
Hume's induction problem says there is no
non-circular reason to expect the

98
00:04:40,057 --> 00:04:41,456
unobserved to resemble the observed.

99
00:04:41,506 --> 00:04:44,355
Saying it has so far assumes the thing in
question.

100
00:04:44,405 --> 00:04:48,123
For example, a résumé screener assumes
next year's applicants will look like the

101
00:04:48,173 --> 00:04:49,282
ones it has seen.

102
00:04:49,332 --> 00:04:53,920
Its only reason is that they always have,
and that is exactly what is in doubt.

103
00:04:54,270 --> 00:04:57,358
The square is the world, the box inside it
a sample.

104
00:04:57,408 --> 00:05:01,066
Complementarity means a sample and the
region it excludes always split the same

105
00:05:01,116 --> 00:05:01,351
world.

106
00:05:01,401 --> 00:05:05,344
Here the sample covers fifty-two point
nine percent, and what it excludes is the

107
00:05:05,394 --> 00:05:05,630
rest.

108
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:09,052
For example, a fit-scoring agent learns
from a few big job boards.

109
00:05:09,102 --> 00:05:10,479
Those listings are its sample.

110
00:05:10,529 --> 00:05:15,328
Jobs posted only on a company's own site,
or passed on by word of mouth, are the

111
00:05:15,378 --> 00:05:18,180
excluded part, and together they make the
whole job market.

112
00:05:18,530 --> 00:05:22,893
The sample grows to ninety-one point one
percent, and its red edge is still there.

113
00:05:22,943 --> 00:05:25,834
Categorical boundary is the sample's edge,
where the gap sits.

114
00:05:25,884 --> 00:05:27,599
Scaling moves it, never removes it.

115
00:05:27,649 --> 00:05:32,306
Structural limit means the limit is built
into what a sample is, not an obstacle to

116
00:05:32,356 --> 00:05:32,895
engineer around.

117
00:05:32,945 --> 00:05:36,131
For example, a job agent adds every board
it can find.

118
00:05:36,181 --> 00:05:39,072
Its boundary moves out to jobs never
posted at all.

119
00:05:39,122 --> 00:05:41,720
No better engineering puts unposted jobs
into posted data.

120
00:05:42,070 --> 00:05:45,420
A running average as observations are
added, in a simulated run.

121
00:05:45,470 --> 00:05:49,439
Mediocristan is a world where no single
outlier dominates the average, such as

122
00:05:49,489 --> 00:05:49,748
heights.

123
00:05:49,798 --> 00:05:53,766
The dark line stays flat, because one more
observation barely moves the mean.

124
00:05:53,816 --> 00:05:57,166
For example, the typing speed of
applicants for a data-entry job.

125
00:05:57,216 --> 00:06:02,112
Even the fastest typist can only type so
fast, so no single applicant can make the

126
00:06:02,162 --> 00:06:02,730
average jump.

127
00:06:03,080 --> 00:06:05,771
The red line is heavy-tailed draws, and it
lurches.

128
00:06:05,821 --> 00:06:10,339
Extremistan is a world where one outlier
can outweigh all the rest, such as wealth.

129
00:06:10,389 --> 00:06:14,603
In this simulated run the largest single
draw is thirty-two point four percent of

130
00:06:14,653 --> 00:06:15,212
the total.

131
00:06:15,262 --> 00:06:17,953
For example, how many applications one job
posting draws.

132
00:06:18,003 --> 00:06:22,826
Most get a modest pile, but a posting that
spreads widely can draw more than many

133
00:06:22,876 --> 00:06:23,740
ordinary postings combined.

134
00:06:24,090 --> 00:06:28,136
A turkey's confidence that it is fed
tomorrow, counted from days fed so far.

135
00:06:28,186 --> 00:06:32,233
The turkey problem is confidence that
rises every day on evidence that is about

136
00:06:32,283 --> 00:06:32,818
to fail.

137
00:06:32,868 --> 00:06:35,744
By day one thousand it is ninety-nine
point nine percent.

138
00:06:35,794 --> 00:06:38,670
For a job-search version, picture someone
who knows some Python.

139
00:06:38,720 --> 00:06:42,766
For years that reliably got people entry-
level programming jobs, and each good year

140
00:06:42,816 --> 00:06:43,937
made it look surer.

141
00:06:43,987 --> 00:06:46,570
Then AI coding agents arrived, and the
world changed.

142
00:06:46,920 --> 00:06:50,816
On day one thousand and one, the red dot,
the turkey is not fed.

143
00:06:50,866 --> 00:06:53,072
Its model gave that zero point one
percent.

144
00:06:53,122 --> 00:06:57,582
A black swan is a rare event that no data
from inside the familiar pattern could

145
00:06:57,632 --> 00:06:58,146
have anticipated.

146
00:06:58,196 --> 00:07:01,246
For example, a job-market model learned
from years of steady hiring.

147
00:07:01,296 --> 00:07:04,629
Then a sudden shock shuts hiring across a
whole industry within weeks.

148
00:07:04,679 --> 00:07:07,730
Nothing in its years of data could have
shown it coming.

149
00:07:08,080 --> 00:07:11,690
Two bell curves, class A and class B,
split by a threshold.

150
00:07:11,740 --> 00:07:15,960
A validation set is held-out data from the
same distribution as the training data,

151
00:07:16,010 --> 00:07:17,180
used to test performance.

152
00:07:17,230 --> 00:07:20,535
In this worked example with made-up
inputs, it scores ninety-four percent.

153
00:07:20,585 --> 00:07:24,805
For example, a model that predicts who
gets an interview is built from past

154
00:07:24,855 --> 00:07:26,330
applications, with some held back.

155
00:07:26,380 --> 00:07:29,380
Its score on those held-back applications
is its validation score.

156
00:07:29,730 --> 00:07:32,384
Both curves move right and the threshold
stays put.

157
00:07:32,434 --> 00:07:36,590
Distribution shift means the inputs met in
deployment drift away from the data the

158
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:37,491
model learned on.

159
00:07:37,541 --> 00:07:39,594
Accuracy drops to seventy-five point nine
percent.

160
00:07:39,644 --> 00:07:41,096
The system did not change.

161
00:07:41,146 --> 00:07:44,401
For example, an interview predictor
learned when applicants wrote their own

162
00:07:44,451 --> 00:07:44,701
résumés.

163
00:07:44,751 --> 00:07:49,508
Now many use AI tools to tailor them, the
résumés it sees look different, and the

164
00:07:49,558 --> 00:07:51,010
same model gets more wrong.

165
00:07:51,360 --> 00:07:53,332
Accuracy against the size of the shift.

166
00:07:53,382 --> 00:07:56,220
The validation set tests only the first
point, shift zero.

167
00:07:56,270 --> 00:07:58,242
Deployment is the red point, seventy-six
percent.

168
00:07:58,292 --> 00:08:02,574
Wrong tests are scoped to the question the
system answers, not the question the world

169
00:08:02,624 --> 00:08:02,863
asks.

170
00:08:02,913 --> 00:08:07,196
For example, a résumé screener is tested
on whether it ranks résumés the way past

171
00:08:07,246 --> 00:08:08,640
recruiters did, and it passes.

172
00:08:08,690 --> 00:08:12,395
The world asks whether the people it ranks
high can do the job.

173
00:08:12,445 --> 00:08:13,550
No test asked that.

174
00:08:13,900 --> 00:08:17,066
A judge compares two equally fluent
transcripts, one human, one machine.

175
00:08:17,116 --> 00:08:21,453
In this simulated run the judge is right
in fifty-seven of one hundred trials, a

176
00:08:21,503 --> 00:08:21,745
guess.

177
00:08:21,795 --> 00:08:25,254
Turing's argument says if behavior is
enough evidence for human minds, demanding

178
00:08:25,304 --> 00:08:26,716
more of machines is unprincipled.

179
00:08:26,766 --> 00:08:30,517
For example, a recruiter runs a chat
screening and cannot tell which candidate

180
00:08:30,567 --> 00:08:32,856
is a person and which an AI agent.

181
00:08:32,906 --> 00:08:36,950
If the answers count as evidence for the
person, they count for the agent.

182
00:08:37,300 --> 00:08:40,077
A room that answers by rulebook, symbols
in, symbols out.

183
00:08:40,127 --> 00:08:41,773
Five of five match, none understood.

184
00:08:41,823 --> 00:08:45,730
The Chinese Room is Searle's argument that
following rules on symbols can look like

185
00:08:45,780 --> 00:08:46,860
understanding without being it.

186
00:08:46,910 --> 00:08:49,404
Whether today's systems do only this is
left open.

187
00:08:49,454 --> 00:08:53,362
For example, picture someone who speaks no
German filling in a German job application

188
00:08:53,412 --> 00:08:56,471
by matching each field to a phrase from a
rule sheet.

189
00:08:56,521 --> 00:08:59,580
Every box comes out right, and none of it
is understood.

190
00:08:59,930 --> 00:09:03,402
Dark is what each argument claimed, red
what it is credited with.

191
00:09:03,452 --> 00:09:06,925
Overclaiming is crediting either argument
with a stronger claim than it made.

192
00:09:06,975 --> 00:09:09,566
Turing proposed a method, not that AI is
intelligent.

193
00:09:09,616 --> 00:09:12,795
Searle showed behavior does not prove
understanding, not that AI cannot

194
00:09:12,845 --> 00:09:13,088
understand.

195
00:09:13,138 --> 00:09:17,491
For example, a vendor says its interview
bot passed a Turing test, so it truly

196
00:09:17,541 --> 00:09:18,078
understands candidates.

197
00:09:18,128 --> 00:09:20,720
That credits Turing with a claim he never
made.

198
00:09:21,070 --> 00:09:24,252
Four boxes, test behavior or not, invoke
the limits or not.

199
00:09:24,302 --> 00:09:26,897
The operational stance is the red box, do
both.

200
00:09:26,947 --> 00:09:28,660
Testing behavior alone misses the limits.

201
00:09:28,710 --> 00:09:30,717
Invoking the limits alone skips the
testing.

202
00:09:30,767 --> 00:09:35,124
For example, before a career office trusts
a cover-letter agent, staff run it on real

203
00:09:35,174 --> 00:09:36,887
job postings and check every letter.

204
00:09:36,937 --> 00:09:41,295
They also ask where its data stops, such
as jobs unlike any it has seen.

205
00:09:41,345 --> 00:09:42,470
That is doing both.

206
00:09:42,820 --> 00:09:47,321
Deployments placed by how much of the
world the data misses and the cost of an

207
00:09:47,371 --> 00:09:47,606
error.

208
00:09:47,656 --> 00:09:49,313
Positions and threshold are made up.

209
00:09:49,363 --> 00:09:53,011
Below the threshold, a product line, where
skepticism as method means doubt is

210
00:09:53,061 --> 00:09:54,433
routine work, verify, monitor, calibrate.

211
00:09:54,483 --> 00:09:57,562
For example, an agent reformats a résumé
into a clean template.

212
00:09:57,612 --> 00:10:02,398
The gap is small and a mistake is cheap
and easy to see, so doubt means checking

213
00:10:02,448 --> 00:10:03,820
the result and moving on.

214
00:10:04,170 --> 00:10:07,208
Five more deployments sit above the
threshold, clinical, driving, judicial

215
00:10:07,257 --> 00:10:08,751
risk, agents and open chat.

216
00:10:08,801 --> 00:10:12,765
Skepticism as safety means that where the
limits bite, the supervisor's doubt is

217
00:10:12,815 --> 00:10:13,691
itself the safeguard.

218
00:10:13,741 --> 00:10:16,470
In this made-up layout that is five of
six.

219
00:10:16,520 --> 00:10:19,866
For example, a screener rejects applicants
before any person sees them.

220
00:10:19,916 --> 00:10:24,806
The people it gets wrong never find out
why, and no metric shows them, so the

221
00:10:24,856 --> 00:10:26,350
recruiter's doubt is the safeguard.

222
00:10:26,700 --> 00:10:31,380
The Epic Sepsis Model is a real sepsis
alert tool that did far worse in outside

223
00:10:31,430 --> 00:10:32,267
validation than marketed.

224
00:10:32,317 --> 00:10:34,928
It missed many cases and flooded
clinicians with alerts.

225
00:10:34,978 --> 00:10:37,884
The dots and squares show that shape with
made-up counts.

226
00:10:37,934 --> 00:10:42,614
The same pattern in job search would be a
résumé screener that looks fine on its

227
00:10:42,664 --> 00:10:46,162
vendor's own data, then, at a new
employer, misses many strong applicants

228
00:10:46,212 --> 00:10:47,640
and flags many weak ones.

229
00:10:47,990 --> 00:10:51,071
Each line is one case, timed from the
blood culture order.

230
00:10:51,121 --> 00:10:54,201
In this made-up example, seven of eight
alerts come after it.

231
00:10:54,251 --> 00:10:58,186
A circular signal is a model that learns
to confirm a suspicion the clinician

232
00:10:58,236 --> 00:10:59,609
already has, not to warn.

233
00:10:59,659 --> 00:11:04,162
For example, a model meant to spot strong
applicants early learns to flag the ones a

234
00:11:04,212 --> 00:11:05,301
recruiter has already starred.

235
00:11:05,351 --> 00:11:09,570
Its flags arrive after she decided, so it
confirms her view instead of warning her.

236
00:11:09,920 --> 00:11:13,119
Three conditions for an override, one time
bar and two ticks.

237
00:11:13,169 --> 00:11:16,367
Override infrastructure is the time,
authority and legibility that make an

238
00:11:16,417 --> 00:11:17,253
override a practice.

239
00:11:17,303 --> 00:11:21,388
In this made-up example, five minutes are
needed and eight are available, so all

240
00:11:21,438 --> 00:11:21,978
three hold.

241
00:11:22,028 --> 00:11:24,932
For example, a recruiter can overrule an
AI screener's rejection.

242
00:11:24,982 --> 00:11:29,361
She has time to look again, the authority
to advance a candidate without a manager's

243
00:11:29,411 --> 00:11:32,610
sign-off, and a screen showing what the
screener could not judge.

244
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:34,370
The time bar turns red.

245
00:11:34,420 --> 00:11:38,167
In this made-up example there are two
minutes against five needed, so one

246
00:11:38,217 --> 00:11:38,751
condition fails.

247
00:11:38,801 --> 00:11:43,132
A fictitious override is an override on
paper that staff have no time or standing

248
00:11:43,182 --> 00:11:43,716
to use.

249
00:11:43,766 --> 00:11:46,929
For example, a hiring policy says
recruiters may overrule the screener.

250
00:11:46,979 --> 00:11:51,602
But each recruiter must clear a full queue
of résumés by noon, so no rejection ever

251
00:11:51,652 --> 00:11:52,770
gets a second look.

252
00:11:53,120 --> 00:11:56,778
Validation clears, then a gate asks
whether the limits bite at these stakes.

253
00:11:56,828 --> 00:11:58,205
If no, deploy and monitor.

254
00:11:58,255 --> 00:11:59,060
If yes, stop.

255
00:11:59,110 --> 00:12:03,624
A stop condition is a case where the
limits and the stakes together mean do not

256
00:12:03,674 --> 00:12:03,910
deploy.

257
00:12:03,960 --> 00:12:07,903
For example, a career office is offered an
agent that sends applications in students'

258
00:12:07,953 --> 00:12:09,044
names with no review.

259
00:12:09,094 --> 00:12:12,753
Its data says little about the employers
students target, and a sent application

260
00:12:12,803 --> 00:12:13,608
cannot be recalled.

261
00:12:13,658 --> 00:12:15,320
Together, that says do not deploy.

262
00:12:15,670 --> 00:12:16,796
Proceed, modify, defer, refuse.

263
00:12:16,846 --> 00:12:20,324
In the top set, refuse is struck out,
leaving three of four.

264
00:12:20,374 --> 00:12:23,852
Authority to refuse is the supervisor's
power to refuse deployment, the most

265
00:12:23,902 --> 00:12:25,322
important authority in the system.

266
00:12:25,372 --> 00:12:27,086
Without it, validate always means clear.

267
00:12:27,136 --> 00:12:31,202
For example, an analyst is asked to review
a new screening model before launch.

268
00:12:31,252 --> 00:12:35,318
If she may only approve, adjust or delay
it, the review is a formality.

269
00:12:35,368 --> 00:12:37,670
If she can say no, it means something.

270
00:12:38,020 --> 00:12:40,879
Tasks sorted into tiers, starting with a
column of five.

271
00:12:40,929 --> 00:12:44,079
Delegate freely covers mechanical, well-
defined procedures whose output you can

272
00:12:44,129 --> 00:12:46,988
check by inspection, such as first drafts,
charts and formatting.

273
00:12:47,038 --> 00:12:49,315
If it goes wrong, you can see it.

274
00:12:49,365 --> 00:12:52,806
For example, an agent puts your résumé
into a clean, consistent layout.

275
00:12:52,856 --> 00:12:57,170
If a heading slips or a date goes missing,
you spot it at a glance.

276
00:12:57,520 --> 00:12:59,211
A second column of four tasks.

277
00:12:59,261 --> 00:13:02,693
Delegate and verify covers interpretive
claims, such as why a distribution is

278
00:13:02,743 --> 00:13:04,434
unusual or why values are missing.

279
00:13:04,484 --> 00:13:07,915
The output sounds plausible, so you must
check it against the world.

280
00:13:07,965 --> 00:13:10,817
For example, an agent explains why a
listing suits you.

281
00:13:10,867 --> 00:13:11,977
The reasons sound convincing.

282
00:13:12,027 --> 00:13:16,330
You still check each one against the
actual job and your own record before you

283
00:13:16,380 --> 00:13:17,200
rely on it.

284
00:13:17,550 --> 00:13:20,089
The red column, problem formulation,
plausibility auditing, judgment under

285
00:13:20,139 --> 00:13:21,239
stakes and the sign-off.

286
00:13:21,289 --> 00:13:25,554
Do not delegate means you do this work
yourself, because no system that cannot be

287
00:13:25,604 --> 00:13:28,718
wrong in a way that matters to it can do
it.

288
00:13:28,768 --> 00:13:32,745
For example, deciding what kind of job you
are actually looking for is problem

289
00:13:32,795 --> 00:13:33,033
formulation.

290
00:13:33,083 --> 00:13:35,046
An agent can search once you know.

291
00:13:35,096 --> 00:13:37,060
It should not decide that for you.

292
00:13:37,410 --> 00:13:40,192
Two chains, each from an AI draft to a
sign-off.

293
00:13:40,242 --> 00:13:44,157
Accountability is being answerable for the
cost of being wrong, which only a person

294
00:13:44,207 --> 00:13:44,723
can carry.

295
00:13:44,773 --> 00:13:47,272
As intended, three of four steps have
someone answerable.

296
00:13:47,322 --> 00:13:48,971
As a checkbox, zero of four.

297
00:13:49,021 --> 00:13:53,502
For example, a job seeker who sends a
cover letter an agent drafted is the one

298
00:13:53,552 --> 00:13:55,484
who answers for it in the interview.

299
00:13:55,534 --> 00:13:56,900
The agent answers for nothing.

300
00:13:57,250 --> 00:14:00,904
Two sets of bars from a simulated run, as
training points grow.

301
00:14:00,954 --> 00:14:05,533
Frequent error, on the left, shrinks from
zero point two eight to zero point zero

302
00:14:05,583 --> 00:14:06,150
zero two.

303
00:14:06,200 --> 00:14:10,780
Categorical error, on the right, goes from
zero point seven three to zero point eight

304
00:14:10,830 --> 00:14:11,088
six.

305
00:14:11,138 --> 00:14:12,940
It stays at the sample's edge.

306
00:14:13,290 --> 00:14:16,090
Turing on the left, Searle on the right.

307
00:14:16,140 --> 00:14:18,940
Turing says behavior that fits
intelligence warrants attributing

308
00:14:18,990 --> 00:14:19,297
intelligence.

309
00:14:19,347 --> 00:14:22,503
Searle says behavior that fits
understanding does not entail

310
00:14:22,553 --> 00:14:22,860
understanding.

311
00:14:22,910 --> 00:14:25,353
One is about what evidence is enough.

312
00:14:25,403 --> 00:14:28,560
The other is about what that evidence
cannot prove.

313
00:14:28,910 --> 00:14:31,701
Method on the left, safety mechanism on
the right.

314
00:14:31,751 --> 00:14:34,226
As a method, skepticism verifies, monitors
and calibrates.

315
00:14:34,276 --> 00:14:37,066
As a safety mechanism, the doubt is the
safeguard.

316
00:14:37,116 --> 00:14:41,801
In a made-up layout of six deployments,
one of six sits below the threshold and

317
00:14:41,851 --> 00:14:44,010
five of six at or above it.

318
00:14:44,360 --> 00:14:47,782
Real override on the left, fictitious
override on the right.

319
00:14:47,832 --> 00:14:50,560
A real override has time, authority and
legibility.

320
00:14:50,610 --> 00:14:52,990
A fictitious override exists only on
paper.

321
00:14:53,040 --> 00:14:55,768
In this made-up example both need five
minutes.

322
00:14:55,818 --> 00:14:57,504
The real one has eight.

323
00:14:57,554 --> 00:14:59,240
The fictitious one has two.

324
00:14:59,590 --> 00:15:02,093
A vague claim, then what its exact version
measures.

325
00:15:02,143 --> 00:15:04,645
The vague claim is, make sure it is safe.

326
00:15:04,695 --> 00:15:08,900
The exact version says what the validation
can and cannot test, and lists the regions

327
00:15:08,950 --> 00:15:09,751
with no data.

328
00:15:09,801 --> 00:15:12,020
Here that is forty percent of the range.

329
00:15:12,370 --> 00:15:15,212
The vague claim is, more data will fix it.

330
00:15:15,262 --> 00:15:19,069
The exact version asks whether the failure
is inside the sampled range.

331
00:15:19,119 --> 00:15:22,282
In the simulated run, error inside fell to
almost nothing.

332
00:15:22,332 --> 00:15:24,853
Outside it ended at zero point eight six.

333
00:15:24,903 --> 00:15:26,460
More data changes nothing there.

334
00:15:26,810 --> 00:15:29,432
The vague claim is, the average looks
stable.

335
00:15:29,482 --> 00:15:33,105
The exact version says Mediocristan or
Extremistan, and reports the largest

336
00:15:33,155 --> 00:15:34,775
draw's share of the total.

337
00:15:34,825 --> 00:15:39,450
In the simulated run, zero point one two
percent for heights, thirty-two point four

338
00:15:39,500 --> 00:15:41,120
percent for the heavy tail.

339
00:15:41,470 --> 00:15:44,346
The vague claim is, the AI will check it.

340
00:15:44,396 --> 00:15:46,297
The exact version sorts the work.

341
00:15:46,347 --> 00:15:50,198
Delegate the draft, verify the narrative,
keep problem formulation and the sign-off.

342
00:15:50,248 --> 00:15:54,750
The bars count five tasks to delegate
freely, four to delegate and verify, four

343
00:15:54,800 --> 00:15:56,050
you do not delegate.

344
00:15:56,400 --> 00:15:59,104
The vague claim is, we have human
oversight.

345
00:15:59,154 --> 00:16:04,268
The exact version names who can override,
the minutes they have, and who holds the

346
00:16:04,318 --> 00:16:05,645
authority to refuse deployment.

347
00:16:05,695 --> 00:16:09,776
In the made-up example, five minutes
needed and two available, a fictitious

348
00:16:09,826 --> 00:16:10,120
override.

349
00:16:10,470 --> 00:16:13,373
A checklist to run before you trust a
deployment.

350
00:16:13,423 --> 00:16:17,638
The first check asks whether the
deployment is inside the data's range or

351
00:16:17,688 --> 00:16:18,622
at its boundary.

352
00:16:18,672 --> 00:16:21,247
Inside, errors are frequent and shrink
with scale.

353
00:16:21,297 --> 00:16:24,200
At the categorical boundary, more data
does not help.

354
00:16:24,550 --> 00:16:28,546
The second check asks whether the quantity
belongs to Mediocristan or Extremistan.

355
00:16:28,596 --> 00:16:32,254
In Mediocristan an average over many cases
is a fair guide.

356
00:16:32,304 --> 00:16:37,649
In Extremistan one rare case can outweigh
all the rest, so a high score on routine

357
00:16:37,699 --> 00:16:38,660
cases says little.

358
00:16:39,010 --> 00:16:42,722
The third check asks whether the
validation asked the system's question or

359
00:16:42,772 --> 00:16:43,349
the world's.

360
00:16:43,399 --> 00:16:46,484
A validation set only tests data like the
training data.

361
00:16:46,534 --> 00:16:51,500
After a distribution shift, a pass on that
set may be a pass on wrong tests.

362
00:16:51,850 --> 00:16:56,337
The fourth check asks whether skepticism
is a method here, or the safety mechanism.

363
00:16:56,387 --> 00:17:00,549
Where the gap is small and errors are
cheap, doubt is routine verification.

364
00:17:00,599 --> 00:17:05,086
Where the limits bite and the stakes are
high, the supervisor's doubt is the

365
00:17:05,136 --> 00:17:05,410
safeguard.

366
00:17:05,760 --> 00:17:10,411
The fifth check asks who can override,
with what time, and whether anyone can

367
00:17:10,461 --> 00:17:10,747
refuse.

368
00:17:10,797 --> 00:17:13,770
Look for override infrastructure, meaning
time, authority and legibility.

369
00:17:13,820 --> 00:17:17,128
If the time is missing, it is a fictitious
override.

370
00:17:17,178 --> 00:17:20,150
Then check that someone holds the
authority to refuse.

371
00:17:20,500 --> 00:17:25,047
The last check asks which tier each task
is in, delegate freely, delegate and

372
00:17:25,097 --> 00:17:28,330
verify, or do not delegate, with a person
keeping accountability.

373
00:17:28,380 --> 00:17:29,972
Then ask the exact question.

374
00:17:30,022 --> 00:17:34,240
Not, is it safe, but safe for whom, doing
what, in which world.

375
00:17:35,090 --> 00:17:35,672
Let's recap.

376
00:17:35,722 --> 00:17:39,461
Scaling shrinks frequent errors, not the
categorical limit at the sample's edge.

377
00:17:39,511 --> 00:17:42,303
The system supplies symbols, the
supervisor supplies meaning and

378
00:17:42,353 --> 00:17:42,619
directedness.

379
00:17:42,669 --> 00:17:46,724
A wider sample still has a boundary, and
the data-world gap sits there.

380
00:17:46,774 --> 00:17:49,882
A validation set tests the system's
question, not the world's.

381
00:17:49,932 --> 00:17:53,040
Where the limits bite, the supervisor's
doubt is the safeguard.

382
00:17:53,090 --> 00:17:56,830
Only a person can carry accountability, so
the sign-off is never delegated.

383
00:17:57,180 --> 00:18:00,195
Give me short deployment cases one at a
time.

384
00:18:00,245 --> 00:18:05,983
For each, I must name the exact term that
fits and say why its look-alike is wrong.

385
00:18:06,033 --> 00:18:09,388
Draw from these terms, data-world gap,
categorical boundary, distribution shift,

386
00:18:09,438 --> 00:18:12,453
Extremistan, circular signal, fictitious
override, stop condition, delegate and

387
00:18:12,503 --> 00:18:13,474
verify, and accountability.

388
00:18:13,524 --> 00:18:17,220
Hold back the answer until I reply, then
correct my wording.

389
00:18:17,570 --> 00:18:19,758
Key Terms: The Limits of AI.

390
00:18:19,808 --> 00:18:21,250
At Nik Bear Brown.
